{"name":"Jacob’s Ladder","hex":"#d6b281","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ladder","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A frame, usually portable, of wood, metal, or rope, used for ascent and descent, consisting of two side pieces to which are fastened rungs (cross strips or rounds acting as steps).","A series of stages by which one progresses to a better position."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To arrange or form into a shape of a ladder.","To ascend (a building, a wall, etc.) using a ladder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ladder"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jacob's Ladder","blocks":[{"text":"Jacob's Ladder is a ladder or staircase leading to Heaven, which featured in a dream the biblical Patriarch Jacob experienced during his flight from his brother Esau."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/LZB_in_Flensburg_-_Niederdeutsche_Lutherbibel_von_1574-1580%2C_Bild_11B.JPG/330px-LZB_in_Flensburg_-_Niederdeutsche_Lutherbibel_von_1574-1580%2C_Bild_11B.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob's_Ladder","wikidata":"Q607715"},{"title":"Ladder","blocks":[{"text":"A ladder is a vertical or inclined set of rungs or steps commonly used for climbing or descending. There are two types: rigid ladders that are self-supporting or that may be leaned against a vertical surface such as a wall, and flexible ladders, such as those made of rope or wire rope, that may be hung from the top."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Ladder.webp/330px-Ladder.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladder","wikidata":"Q168639"},{"title":"Jacob","blocks":[{"text":"Jacob, later given the name Israel, is the third Hebrew patriarch in Judaism and an important figure in the Abrahamic religions. He first appears in the Torah, where he is described in the Book of Genesis as a son of Isaac and Rebecca. Accordingly, alongside his older fraternal twin brother Esau, Jacob's paternal grandparents are Abraham and Sarah and his maternal grandfather is Bethuel, whose wife is not mentioned. He is said to have bought Esau's birthright and, with his mother's help, deceived his aging father to bless him instead of Esau. Then, following a severe drought in his homeland Canaan, Jacob and his descendants migrated to neighbouring Egypt through the efforts of his son Joseph, who had become a confidant of the pharaoh. After dying in Egypt at the age of 147, he is supposed to have been buried in the Cave of Machpelah in Hebron."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Jan_Victors_-_Jacob_Seeking_the_Forgiveness_of_Esau_-_79.330_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Jan_Victors_-_Jacob_Seeking_the_Forgiveness_of_Esau_-_79.330_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob","wikidata":"Q289957"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Monheim_Town_Hall_3.jpg/500px-Monheim_Town_Hall_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Monheim_Town_Hall_3.jpg/500px-Monheim_Town_Hall_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jacob's Ladder as depicted in Monheim Town Hall. The gilded Hebrew text reads \"And, behold, the LORD stood beside him, and said: 'And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee whithersoever thou goest'\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMonheim_Town_Hall_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/El_sue%C3%B1o_de_Jacob%2C_por_Jos%C3%A9_de_Ribera.jpg/500px-El_sue%C3%B1o_de_Jacob%2C_por_Jos%C3%A9_de_Ribera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig